re they are  so if you're a complete beginner you can  take these classes you read the texts we  meet we discuss them and you'll come out  knowing a lot more I'll give you  background information if you're sort of  an intermediate stage learner you've  already read these platonic dialogues  well rereading them and going going over  them again with me you're going to learn  some things most likely that you may  have overlooked before or didn't take  quite as seriously so whatever level  you're coming at it with you'll get  something out of these classes I'd like  to tell you a little bit about each of  the class sessions but I do want to  stress each of the 90-minute class  sessions in general we're going to be  doing a couple things one of them and  perhaps the most important is we will be  dialoguing with the dialogue that's one  of the great things about live  interactions particularly when applied  to platonic dialogues these are texts  that have an established you could say  set in stone way of presenting  themselves but they invite us and this  is part of the genius of Plato  to enter into the situation to ask  questions that haven't been asked to  make connections and to think about the  application of the ideas to our own life  and culture and experiences and  situations so they're not just studying  these taxes if they are you know once  again set in stone and have nothing  other than the the Frozen words to use a  platonic term to communicate to us we're  going to be exploring these seeing what  Socrates actually has to tell us and  then talking a lot about that together  will go over the key ideas some  background assumptions that are being  made we'll talk about the arguments that  Socrates and other people are making  whether they're good whether they're bad  Plato will in fact sometimes give  Socrates bad arguments to work with as  you know a Spur to our own thinking  about this sort of stuff now what are we  going to do in each class session let me  give you a rundown so on November 16th  from 11 to 12 30 central time that's  when all the classes are going to be  Wednesdays 11 to 12 30. we are going to  begin with talking about platonic  dialogues in general and then going into  the ion what is the ion about ion  himself is a Rap Zone somebody who  interprets poetry and specifically The  Poetry of homer so we're going to be  looking at the interaction between him  and Socrates where they discuss whether  the poets or the rap zones the  interpreters actually know anything or  whether they're kind of winging it and  if they are where are their ideas coming  from so that leads us to another key  idea poetry or cultural production as  divinely inspired and we'll talk about  that but we're also going to talk about  speaking well and speaking poorly and  you'll get to see how Socrates makes the  poets and the rap Zones look like they  don't really know what they're talking  about which is part of what leads to the  accusation against him in the apology in  week two we are going to be looking at  the interaction that Socrates has in the  euthyphro right before he goes on trial  euthy fro is bringing up his own father  on charges of murder there's accusations  of impiety Socrates is himself accused  of impiety and that raises the question  what is piety and we see that there's a  lot of ins and outs to that question  some of the interesting you could call  them Sidelines that we're going to go on  have to do with human and divine  conflict we're also going to talk about  the famous euthyphro dilemma and its  importance in philosophy of religion and  then we we will move on in the next week  on November 30th to the apology itself  the defense speech which is what an  apology originally was that Socrates  gives at his trial where he explains his  mission from God the god Apollo and  Socrates own understanding of that going  around questioning people the effects  that that's had in creating old accusers  politicians Crafts People poets writers  who didn't like being shown up as not  knowing anything  Socrates I'll give you a little spoiler  here is going to be condemned to death  and that his friends stick around and he  explains why death isn't necessarily the  bad thing that we make it out to be and  he gives some arguments for that so  that's the end of week three  week four on December 7th we will be  looking at The Credo and this is a  dialogue that is taking place right  before Socrates is supposed to be  executed his friend Credo says I've  bribed the guards let's get the hell out  of here and Socrates says  let's consider this would that be the  right thing to do Socrates as you can  tell decides that it's not and the  argumentation about why that isn't the  right thing to do even though he was  unjustly accused and unjustly sentenced  that turns out to be a very important uh  platonic idea and he'll even bring in  some other characters the laws of Athens  then the last two weeks of our course on  December 14th and December 21st we're  going to be looking at one of the  greatest platonic dialogues ever written  the Fado this is taking place on the day  of Socrates own execution in the  Athenian jail his friends are there they  send away the mourners and he says let's  talk let's spend the rest of the time  that we have doing philosophy together  and what do they do they discuss why  philosophy is a preparation for death  they also go into the difference between  philosophical virtues and ordinary  people's mistaken conception of the  virtues we cover some other important  topics like why we shouldn't be not only  misanthropists but missologists and then  they get into the real meat and that's  what we'll be talking about our final  class session as we wrap up  are there Arguments for and against  the existence of the Soul after the body  dies is the soul Immortal is do we  continue on after death or not  and Socrates you know he's considering  this as a very important philosophical  question but it's also existentially  important for him because he is going to  die that day by the very end of the  dialogue so those are the things that  we're going to be covering and it's a  great sort of selection of some of the  the key arguments and ideas and and  commitments and background assumptions  to platonic philosophy after you've  studied this I think you can go on to  other dialogues and get a lot more out  of them I hope that you'll join me for  this class as you can tell I'm very  passionate about this I love engaging in  online learning especially live where  the questions come you know thick and  fast and we got to think our way through  them together along you know an  instructor along with students so you  see the link below if you want to check  the class pass out and see what we've  got going on in it you can click it  it'll take you right to the light Hall  platform and I hope that some of you  will join me for this class on six uh  sessions five platonic dialogues we're  gonna have a great time together and  I'll see some of you there
 

